iminate lower numbers. However, I hold W1\u00d72 and W2, which conflict with Upper Tiles. Alternatively, **Outside Hand (4 pts)** is feasible due to terminals and honors absence.\n\nKey observation: W5 is completely gone (0 left), meaning no one can use W5 for chows (e.g., 4-5-6). This makes W4 and W6 more valuable. But W6 is already punged, so W4 might be safer to keep for 3-4-5 or 4-5-6 (but W5=0 \u2192 impossible). So W4 cannot form 3-4-5 or 4-5-6. Hence, **W4 is relatively useless** unless someone uses it in a 2-3-4 or standalone pung.\n\nSimilarly, W2 and W1 could form 1-2-3 chow. But W3 is available (4 unshown), so possible.\n\nBest strategic direction: Aim for **Full Flush** in Characters. Discard non-Character tiles: **T1 and B2/B7/B9** are candidates.\n\nBut valid actions include both T1 and B-tiles. Among them:\n- **T1**: Only one; suit different from main hand. Discarding it reduces mixed-suit clutter.\n- **B2, B7, B9**: All Bamboo; holding four Bamboo tiles hurts flush potential. Must discard them eventually.\n\nHowever, **B7** was just used in previous player\u2019s chow [B5,B6,B7] \u2014 very dangerous to discard! Similarly, **B3** has been played thrice \u2014 likely someone has a pung or is collecting. But **B7** was just completed \u2014 so probably safe? Wait: Previous player *used* B7 via \"Chi B6\" \u2192 actually, they formed [B5,B6,B7], meaning they claimed B7? No: \u201cChi B6\u201d means they completed [B5,B6,B7] using their own B5+B6 and discarded B7? No \u2014 clarification: \"Chi X\" means they used the discarded tile to complete a chow ending or centered on X.\n\nWait: \u201cChi B6\u201d \u2014 based on notation rule: 'Chi B6' means the player formed the chow [B5, B6, B7] by claiming the discarded B6. So they had B5 and B7 in hand, and picked up B6. Therefore, **B6 was discarded**, and they used it. So **B6 is dangerous**, but **B7 is safe**? Not necessarily \u2014 they may still be collecting B7.\n\nBut they later discarded B3 and T8 \u2014 unclear.\n\nMore importantly: They had [B5,B6,B7] as a meld, so now they possess that set. Likely they don\u2019t need B7 anymore. Still, caution.\n\nBut among my discards, **T1** stands out as the only non-Character, non-Bamboo-terminal. It's a single tile in a different suit, and T1 has already been played by others (across player played T1 twice). So likely safe.\n\nAlternatively, **B2** \u2014 no one has shown interest in low Bamboos. B3 has been played three times, possibly indicating someone has a pung (next player has [B3,B4,B5]). So B2 could complete a chow for them (1-2-3 or 2-3-4). Since they have B3 and B4, **B2 is dangerous** \u2014 could complete [B2,B3,B4].\n\nSimilarly, **B7**: previous player has [B5,B6,B7], so B7 is part of their set \u2014 they may discard extra B7? But unlikely. Still, they already have it, so probably don\u2019t need more. But better safe than sorry.\n\n**B9**: only two unshown besides my two. If I discard one, only one remains. Possible that someone is collecting terminals. Across player has [T1,T2,T3] and [W6,W6,W6], not terminal-heavy. But hard to say.\n\nBack to strategy: To maximize score potential, I should aim for **Full Flush (24 pts)** + possibly **Upper Tiles (24 pts)** if I can shift focus. But I have W1\u00d72, W2, W4\u00d72 \u2014 too many low tiles. So **Upper Tiles is unlikely**.\n\nBetter: go for **Outside Hand (4 pts)** by keeping terminals and honors. But I have no honor tiles. So need to either get one or abandon.\n\nAlternatively, **All Chows (2 pts)** is possible if I build chows in W. I have W1, W2, W4 \u2014 could form 1-2-3 (need W3), 2-3-4 (need W3), 4-5-6 (W5=0 \u2192 impossible). So W4 cannot form chow without W5. Thus, **W4 is dead tile**.\n\nSame for W7: can form 5-6-7 (W5=0 \u2192 impossible), 6-7-8 (W6=1 left), 7-8-9 (I have W7, W8, W9 \u2014 yes! W7, W8, W9 all present).\n\nI have W7\u00d72, W8, W9 \u2014 so I can form **[W7,W8,W9]** chow. That\u2019s excellent.\n\nAlso, W1\u00d72, W2 \u2014 could form [W1,W2,W3] if I draw W3.\n\nSo viable chow structure: [W1,W2,W3], [W7,W8,W9], and another pair.\n\nAlso, W4\u00d72 \u2014 isolated, no W5, so cannot form chow. W4 is useless.\n\nTherefore, **W4 is the worst tile in hand** \u2014 cannot form any chow, not terminal, not useful for pung (only two, and no third likely), and blocks progress.\n\nMoreover, W4 unshown: 4 copies still exist \u2014 unlikely to be rare, but no way to use.\n\nThus, best discard: **W4**\n\nOther options:\n- **W2**: part of potential [W1,W2,W3], so better kept.\n- **W1**: same.\n- **W7/W8/W9**: core of upper chow.\n- **B-tiles**: necessary to discard eventually, but W4 is worse because it\u2019s in my dominant suit but unusable.\n\nHence, **discard W4** to move toward a chow-based hand in Characters, aiming for **All Chows (2 pts)** + possible **Pure Double Chow (1 pt)** if I get two identical chows, or **Short Straight (2 pts)** if I get 1-2-3 and 7-8-9.\n\nAlso, since I\u2019m drawing and discarding, avoiding giving useful tiles.\n\nReason: W4 cannot form any chow due to W5 being exhausted, and it doesn't contribute to terminal/honor patterns. It is the least useful tile in hand. Discarding it improves hand flexibility toward viable chows like [W1,W2,W3] and [W7,W8,W9].\n\nAnswer: Play W4"}
