es.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **Play T4**: Removes a non-Character tile, pushing toward a Character-only hand. Low risk since T4 has 2 remaining and one already played; likely safe.\n- **Play T6**: Similar reasoning \u2014 isolated tile, no chow possible (T5 gone), helps streamline toward Full Flush.\n- **Play T7**: Part of a potential T6-T7-T8 chow, but T8 and T5 are gone, so no completion possible. Also, keeping T7 doesn\u2019t help long-term plan. Better to discard than keep.\n- **Play B1/B9**: Both are singletons from other suits; removing them supports a pure Character hand. However, B9 completes my [B7,B8,B9] meld, so it's already used; discarding B1 or B9 now doesn't harm structure.\n- **Play W1/W7/W8/W9**: These are core to my potential winning hands (Full Flush, Upper Tiles, All Terminals). Discarding any would weaken these patterns. W1 pair might be misleading \u2014 hard to use unless going for All Terminals, which is unlikely.\n\nBest strategic direction: Aim for **Full Flush (24 points)** in Characters. I already have 6 Character tiles: W1, W1, W7, W7, W8, W9. If I can draw W7, W8, or W9 (many still available), I could form additional chows or pungs. Remaining tiles like W7 (4 left), W8 (2), W9 (2) are accessible.\n\nTo pursue this, I must discard non-Character tiles: T4, T6, T7, B1, B9.\n\nAmong these, **T4** and **T6** are safest to discard because:\n- T5 is exhausted \u2192 no one can complete a chow with T4 or T6.\n- T4 was just discarded by opponent (previous player\u2019s history) \u2192 likely safe.\n- T6 has 3 copies unshown, but none drawn yet \u2014 but again, T5 gone blocks most chows.\n\nHowever, **T7** is more dangerous \u2014 T6 and T8 are both out, so someone may be completing T6-T7-T8. But T8 and T9 are already melded by others ([T8,T8,T8] exposed), so T7 is less useful to others.\n\nStill, among the non-W tiles, **T4** is the weakest link: no sequence possible, not a terminal, and least likely to contribute.\n\nBut wait \u2014 looking at valid actions, I must choose one of:  \n[Play T7; Play W8; Play T6; Play B9; Play W7; Play W9; Play W1; Play B1; Play T4]\n\nFinal decision factors:\n- Keep W7 pair (potential pung, worth Dragon Pung equivalent in value via Pung of Terminals/Honors \u00d72).\n- Keep W8/W9 for Upper Tiles or Full Flush.\n- W1 pair could be useful, but W1 has 2 remaining \u2014 not rare, but not gone.\n- Best discard: **T4** \u2014 it's a mid-game tile in a broken suit, cannot form chow, and hinders flush pursuit.\n\nReason: Discarding **T4** commits to a Character-based hand (Full Flush or Upper Tiles), removes a tile with no future utility, and avoids breaking up valuable pairs or sequences in W. It minimizes loss of potential patterns while maximizing safety and focus.\n\nAnswer: Play T4"}
